# What promoting a concatenation, a comparison, a logical operator or a
# prefix operator has to keep, by kind.
#
# **Shapes**, which ask what the operator answers: every concatenation
# spelling, both comparison families, every logical operator, each of the
# prefix operators `+`, `-` and `\`, and an operand that is itself an operator
# -- including a prefix over a prefix and a prefix over a binary operator's
# result, which is where an operand register holds an intermediate rather than
# a read.
#
# **Settings**, which is where a comparison reads state a concatenation does
# not. `NUMERIC DIGITS` rounds the operands before they are compared, so
# narrowing it makes two different numbers equal; `NUMERIC FUZZ` widens that
# further by subtracting from the working precision. A strict comparison reads
# neither -- it compares bytes -- and the rows put the two side by side under
# the same settings so that one row says which family read what.
# The prefix `-` reads `NUMERIC DIGITS` too, and that is what says it is `0 -
# x` rather than a sign flip on the operand's own digits: at DIGITS 1 the
# oracle answers `-1E+4` for `-12345` and the identical `-1E+4` for `0 -
# 12345`. A `\` row at the same setting is that stanza's control, because the
# text check reads no setting at all.
#
# **Failure**, which for these operators is nearly all in the operands.
# Concatenation renders both operands and cannot itself raise; a comparison
# falls back to a string compare rather than failing when an operand will not
# convert. The raise a binary operator here makes of its own is the logical
# family's 34.901, and its rows pin both halves of the measured rule: **both
# operands are checked even when the first has already decided the answer**,
# and when both are bad it is the **left** operand's text that is reported.
# The prefix operators are the other way round -- the failure is the
# operator's, over its one operand -- and `\` and `-` fail *differently* on
# the same operand: `\` checks text and gives 34.901 where `-` converts and
# gives 41.1, so the rows put `'abc'` under each.
#
# **Trace**, which is where a native op diverges first and where no other
# instrument in this crate can see it. `eval.rs` emits a binary operator's
# `>O>` line and a prefix operator's `>P>` line as a side effect of evaluating
# them; an op that only computes into
# a register emits nothing, and every line after the missing one still matches
# -- so only an exact stderr comparison catches it. Both directions are here:
# a `trace i` row requires the lines, and a `trace r` row requires their
# absence. The `trace i` row carries the abuttal, whose tag is the empty
# string, and the blank, whose tag is one space -- tags an implementation
# reading a spelling off the source span would get wrong and no other row can
# see. **A prefix operator's line is `>P>` and never `>O>`**, and those bytes
# are already pinned for the tree-walker by `tests/trace_oracle`'s own
# `prefix_operators` witness, which runs on that engine alone. What the `trace
# i` prefix row adds is the compiled clause: that an op stream emits the line
# at all, and where it falls among the operand's own lines.
#
# **What this file is worth was measured rather than assumed, and it is not
# mutation-catching.** Four mutations of the promoted binary path were each
# re-run with this file moved out of the directory, and every one was caught
# anyway:
# dropping the `>O>` echo behind the new op reddens `calls`, whose own row
# holds a blank operator; checking a logical operator's right operand before
# its left reddens a unit test written for that rule; a comparison that
# ignores `NUMERIC FUZZ` reddens another; and `|` computing exclusive-or --
# the one the operand-combination rows below were written for, because this
# file's own logical stanza could not see it before them -- reddens
# `assertions.rs`'s `the_exempt_set_matches_the_current_blocked_rows`, which
# runs rows extracted from ooTest rather than anything written here: seven of
# those rows stop passing, so the not-passing set no longer matches the
# committed exempt list and that test's own equality fails.
#
# The row written to close that blind spot did not make the file a sole
# catcher even for the mutation it closes -- and these rows do not have to be
# sole catchers to earn their place.
#
# **So the reason to keep these rows is that they pin oracle bytes, which is
# something an engine-against-engine comparison structurally cannot do**: both
# arms of that comparison are this crate, so a shared path that is wrong the
# same way twice passes it, and a unit test says what this crate should print
# rather than what the C++ interpreter does.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE, and every row
# was measured to produce the identical bytes on both engines before these
# operators were promoted as well as after -- which is what says a row can
# tell whether it would ever have failed. Regenerate a row by running its
# program under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 -- ir_dual.rs refuses to run
# at all under REWRITE for that reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )
#
# run from a fresh empty directory: the scratchpad is on the oracle's
# external-routine search path and a program calling an unresolved name finds
# a stale file there and runs it.
#
# Expected lines are tagged so that no line of the block can be empty: an empty
# line ends an expected block, and a program's own output may hold one.
# Trailing blanks are part of what is compared.

# every concatenation spelling, over the same two operands: `||`, the
# abuttal between two terms with nothing between them, and the blank between
# two terms with whitespace. **The blank inserts exactly one space however much
# whitespace separated the terms** -- `say za zb` is written twice, once with
# one space and once with four, and the two answer identically, because the
# scanner has collapsed that distinction before the parser sees it.
# The last rows chain the spellings, which is what makes one join's
# result the next one's operand register.
program
za = 'p'
zb = 'q'
say za || zb
say za'r'
say za zb
say za    zb
say 'a' || za || zb
say za || zb 'r'
say za'r'zb
----
rc> 0
out> pq
out> pr
out> p q
out> p q
out> apq
out> pq r
out> prq

# both comparison families over the same operands. The non-strict one
# compares numerically when both sides convert (`'1' = '1.0'`) and drops to a
# string compare when either does not (`'ABC' < 'abd'`); the strict one
# compares bytes always, so it separates `1` from `1.0` and keeps the blank in
# `' 1'` that the numeric compare ignores.
program
za = '1'
zb = '1.0'
say za = zb
say za == zb
say ' 1' = 1
say ' 1' == 1
say 'ABC' < 'abd'
say 'ABC' >> 'abd'
say za \= zb
say za \== zb
say 2 <> 3
say 2 >< 2
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 0
out> 1
out> 1
out> 0

# NUMERIC DIGITS narrowed, which a comparison reads and a concatenation does
# not: the operands are rounded to the precision before they are compared, so
# at DIGITS 4 `1.0001` and `1` are equal and at DIGITS 9 they are not. **The
# strict rows are the control and there is one at each setting**, which is what
# says the setting cannot move a byte comparison -- a single strict row would
# only have said the families differ where it sat.
program
numeric digits 4
say 1.0001 = 1
say 1.001 = 1
say 1.0001 == 1
numeric digits 9
say 1.0001 = 1
say 1.0001 == 1
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0

# NUMERIC FUZZ, the other setting a comparison reads: it subtracts from the
# precision the comparison works at, so at FUZZ 3 `1000000` and `1000001`
# compare equal, differing in the seventh significant digit, while `1000000`
# and `1000100`, differing in the fifth, do not. FUZZ then goes back to zero at
# the same site, so the rows say the setting is read when the comparison runs
# rather than when the clause was compiled. **The strict rows are the control
# and there is one at each setting**, for the reason the DIGITS stanza above
# gives.
program
numeric fuzz 3
say 1000000 = 1000001
say 1000000 == 1000001
say 1000000 = 1000100
numeric fuzz 0
say 1000000 = 1000001
say 1000000 == 1000001
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0

# each logical operator over every combination of two logical operands, which
# is what makes the rows separate the operators from each other rather than
# only exercise them. `|` and `&&` agree everywhere except on two true
# operands, so that is where the rows telling exclusive-or from inclusive have
# to be: `say 1 && 1` answers `0` and catches `&&` computed as `|`, `say 1 | 1`
# answers `1` and catches `|` computed as `&&`, and each catches its own
# direction alone -- it takes both rows to cover both directions.
program
say 1 & 1
say 1 & 0
say 0 & 1
say 0 & 0
say 1 | 1
say 1 | 0
say 0 | 1
say 0 | 0
say 1 && 1
say 1 && 0
say 0 && 1
say 0 && 0
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0
out> 1
out> 1
out> 1
out> 0
out> 0
out> 1
out> 1
out> 0

# **both operands are checked even when the first has already decided the
# answer**: `0 & 'x'` is false whatever the right operand is, and the oracle
# raises 34.901 on `"x"` all the same. An implementation that short-circuited
# would print `0` here.
program
say (0 & 'x')
----
rc> 222
err>      1 *-* say (0 & 'x')
err> Error 34 running /nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex line 1:  Logical value not 0 or 1.
err> Error 34.901:  Logical value must be exactly "0" or "1"; found "x".

# both operands bad, where the substitution says which one was checked first:
# the report names `"y"`, the **left** operand's own text.
program
say ('y' & 'x')
----
rc> 222
err>      1 *-* say ('y' & 'x')
err> Error 34 running /nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex line 1:  Logical value not 0 or 1.
err> Error 34.901:  Logical value must be exactly "0" or "1"; found "y".

# a failure reached through each family, trapped, so that one row says which
# clause SIGL lands on for each. Concatenation and comparison have no raise of
# their own -- a concatenation renders whatever it is given and a comparison
# falls back to a string compare -- so what fails inside them is an operand,
# and the clause the condition is reported against is the whole `SAY`'s. The
# logical row is the operator's own 34.901 beside them.
program
signal on syntax name z1
say (1/0) = 1
z1:
say 'compare' rc 'at' sigl
signal on syntax name z2
say 'a' || (1/0)
z2:
say 'concat' rc 'at' sigl
signal on syntax name z3
say ('y' & 'x')
z3:
say 'logical' rc 'at' sigl
----
rc> 0
out> compare 42 at 2
out> concat 42 at 6
out> logical 34 at 10

# an operand that is itself an operator, which is what puts an intermediate in
# a compiled operand register rather than a read. Precedence decides which is
# the inner one: concatenation binds tighter than comparison, so
# `say (za || zb) = 'pq'` and `say za || zb = 'pq'` agree without the
# parentheses mattering.
program
za = 'p'
zb = 'q'
say (za || zb) = 'pq'
say za || zb = 'pq'
say 1 = 1 & 2 = 2
say ('a' 'b') || ('c' 'd')
say (za = 'p') && (zb = 'q')
say za || zb || za zb
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 1
out> 1
out> a bc d
out> 0
out> pqp q

# trace i: the `>O>` line each operator's result owes, after both operands' own
# lines and in the order the operators are applied -- the inner `>` and `<`
# before the outer `&`. The tags are the operators' own spellings, including
# the abuttal's empty string and the blank's single space.
program
trace i
za = 2
zb = 'q'
zc = za || zb
say zc = '2q'
say za'r'
say za zb
say za > 1 & za < 9
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 2r
out> 2 q
out> 1
err>      2 *-* za = 2
err>        >L>   "2"
err>        >>>   "2"
err>        >=>   ZA <= "2"
err>      3 *-* zb = 'q'
err>        >L>   "q"
err>        >>>   "q"
err>        >=>   ZB <= "q"
err>      4 *-* zc = za || zb
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >V>   ZB => "q"
err>        >O>   "||" => "2q"
err>        >>>   "2q"
err>        >=>   ZC <= "2q"
err>      5 *-* say zc = '2q'
err>        >V>   ZC => "2q"
err>        >L>   "2q"
err>        >O>   "=" => "1"
err>        >>>   "1"
err>      6 *-* say za'r'
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >L>   "r"
err>        >O>   "" => "2r"
err>        >>>   "2r"
err>      7 *-* say za zb
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >V>   ZB => "q"
err>        >O>   " " => "2 q"
err>        >>>   "2 q"
err>      8 *-* say za > 1 & za < 9
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >L>   "1"
err>        >O>   ">" => "1"
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >L>   "9"
err>        >O>   "<" => "1"
err>        >O>   "&" => "1"
err>        >>>   "1"

# trace r: the other direction. An emission that ignored its own gate would
# print a `>O>` line here, where the setting asks for results alone.
program
trace r
za = 2
zc = za || 'x'
say zc = '2x'
----
rc> 0
out> 1
err>      2 *-* za = 2
err>        >>>   "2"
err>      3 *-* zc = za || 'x'
err>        >>>   "2x"
err>      4 *-* say zc = '2x'
err>        >>>   "1"

# each prefix operator over a read and over a literal, which is the whole of
# what one applied to a plain term answers. The two rows that could distinguish
# a sign flip on the operand's own digits from the subtraction `-` actually is
# are `-0`, which answers `0` and not `-0`, and `-'7.50'`, which keeps the
# operand's trailing zero -- measured, `say 0 - 0` and `say 0 - 7.50` give the
# identical `0` and `-7.50`.
program
za = 3
zb = 0
say +za
say -za
say \zb
say +5
say -5
say \1
say -0
say +'7'
say -'7.50'
----
rc> 0
out> 3
out> -3
out> 1
out> 5
out> -5
out> 0
out> 0
out> 7
out> -7.50

# a prefix over something other than a plain term: over another prefix, over a
# binary operator's result, and as a binary operator's own operand -- which is
# what puts an intermediate in a compiled operand register rather than a read.
# **A prefix takes a whole message subterm before any dyadic operator is
# considered**, so `-2 ** 2` is 4 and `-za + 1` is `(-za) + 1`; the
# parenthesised rows beside them are the pair that says so, since `-(2 ** 2)`
# answers -4 and `-(za + 1)` answers -4 where the unparenthesised forms answer
# 4 and -2.
program
za = 3
zb = 1
say - -za
say -(-za)
say \\zb
say -(za + 1)
say -(za || za)
say \(za = 3)
say \(za > 9)
say -2 ** 2
say -(2 ** 2)
say -za + 1
say \zb & zb
----
rc> 0
out> 3
out> 3
out> 1
out> -4
out> -33
out> 0
out> 1
out> 4
out> -4
out> -2
out> 0

# NUMERIC DIGITS, which the prefix `-` and `+` read and the prefix `\` does
# not: the operand is rounded to the precision, so at DIGITS 1 a five-digit
# operand comes back in exponential notation and at DIGITS 9 it comes back
# whole. **The `\` row is the control**, and it says the setting cannot reach
# a text check.
program
numeric digits 1
say -12345
say +12345
say \0
numeric digits 9
say -12345
----
rc> 0
out> -1E+4
out> 1E+4
out> 1
out> -12345

# `\` on a value that is not logical: a **text** check and never a numeric one,
# so the report is 34.901 with the operand's own text and not the 41.1 an
# arithmetic conversion would give.
program
say \'abc'
----
rc> 222
err>      1 *-* say \'abc'
err> Error 34 running /nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex line 1:  Logical value not 0 or 1.
err> Error 34.901:  Logical value must be exactly "0" or "1"; found "abc".

# `-` on the same value that is not numeric, which is the row beside the one
# above: the operand is converted first, so this is 41.1 where `\` on the
# identical operand is 34.901. Either row alone would be satisfied by an
# implementation that gave one number to both.
program
say -'abc'
----
rc> 215
err>      1 *-* say -'abc'
err> Error 41 running /nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex line 1:  Bad arithmetic conversion.
err> Error 41.1:  Nonnumeric value ("abc") used in arithmetic operation.

# trace i: the `>P>` line each prefix operator's result owes, after its
# operand's own lines and in the order the operators are applied -- so a prefix
# over a prefix prints the inner one's line first, and a prefix over a binary
# operator prints after that operator's `>O>`. The tags are the operators' own
# spellings, and `\`'s is the one an implementation reading a source span would
# get wrong.
program
trace i
za = 2
zb = -za
say \(za = 3)
say -(za + 1)
say - -za
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> -3
out> 2
err>      2 *-* za = 2
err>        >L>   "2"
err>        >>>   "2"
err>        >=>   ZA <= "2"
err>      3 *-* zb = -za
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >P>   "-" => "-2"
err>        >>>   "-2"
err>        >=>   ZB <= "-2"
err>      4 *-* say \(za = 3)
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >L>   "3"
err>        >O>   "=" => "0"
err>        >P>   "\" => "1"
err>        >>>   "1"
err>      5 *-* say -(za + 1)
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >L>   "1"
err>        >O>   "+" => "3"
err>        >P>   "-" => "-3"
err>        >>>   "-3"
err>      6 *-* say - -za
err>        >V>   ZA => "2"
err>        >P>   "-" => "-2"
err>        >P>   "-" => "2"
err>        >>>   "2"

# trace r: the other direction for `>P>`, which is a gate of its own rather
# than the one the `>O>` row above tests. An emission that ignored it would
# print a `>P>` line here, where the setting asks for results alone.
program
trace r
za = 2
zb = -za
say \(za = 3)
----
rc> 0
out> 1
err>      2 *-* za = 2
err>        >>>   "2"
err>      3 *-* zb = -za
err>        >>>   "-2"
err>      4 *-* say \(za = 3)
err>        >>>   "1"
